    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Six positions, and choosing between them is most of the act of changing key. A phrase that keeps arriving on one of C major's three is being pointed at C; one that keeps arriving on A minor's three, in the exact same five frets, is being pointed at A minor. Nothing about the window decides it — what the phrase leans on and where it stops does, which is the same account of home chapter 1 gave."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "tip",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The dots do not change. The chord underneath and the note you stop on do."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The chords are shared too, and this is new. Every major chord in the key has a minor chord three semitones below it sharing two of its three notes:"
        }
      ]
    },
