    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "So the two keys don't merely share a note set — they share three chord pairs, each pair one note apart. What's "
        },
        {
          "text": "I",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " in C major is "
        },
        {
          "text": "III",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " in A minor; "
        },
        {
          "text": "vi",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " becomes "
        },
        {
          "text": "i",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "; "
        },
        {
          "text": "IV",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " becomes "
        },
        {
          "text": "VI",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "; "
        },
        {
          "text": "V",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " becomes "
        },
        {
          "text": "VII",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Chords A Minor Gives You",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-the-chords-of-the-key"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " owns the full table; this lesson isn't reprinting it. So a chord you already know how to play, sitting under your fingers right now, is simultaneously a chord of both keys — it's the numeral, not the shape, that decides which one you're calling it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the demonstration, and it is sharper than chapter 1's version of the same idea. Take the same four chords and rotate them:"
